The '''economy of [[Okaiken]]''' is a highly developed [[mixed economy|mixed]] [[market economy]], characterized by the doctrine of {{nihongo|"Industrial Sovereignty"|産業主権|Sangyō Shuken}}—a state policy of maintaining domestic control over critical heavy industries while aggressively expanding into the digital frontier. Okaiken is the [[List of countries by GDP (nominal)|6th largest national economy]] in the world by nominal GDP and the 7th largest by [[purchasing power parity]] (PPP). A founding member of the [[East Fosian Sphere]] and a permanent participant in the [[Fosia Summits]], Okaiken was the first nation in Fosia to achieve developed-country status.

The structural foundations of Okaiken's economy were laid during the Imperial period, utilizing the complex network of transport routes and the futures contracts, banking, and insurance systems of the [[Aizawa]] merchant guilds. Following the [[Great Adonian War]], the [[Saito period]] saw Okaiken embrace a guided market economy, leading to the '''Sanese Industrial Miracle'''. Real economic growth averaged 7.5 percent throughout the 1960s and 1970s, establishing the nation as the premier manufacturing hub of Fosia. Growth slowed in the 1990s during the {{nihongo|"Restructuring Era"|再構築時代|Saikōchiku Jidai}}, a period characterized by aggressive government intervention to pivot the economy from light manufacturing to high-technology and heavy industrial automation. Beneath these institutional structures lies an older and more immutable constraint: of Okaiken's 208,566 km², forests cover 65.5% and farmland 16.3%, leaving only 5.8% of the national territory urbanized—a scarcity of buildable land that has made compact, high-value use a structural fact of the Sanese economy.

However, the first half of 2026 saw significant economic disruption. The {{nihongo|"En Crisis"|円危機|En Kiki}} of January–February 2026 saw a sharp 20% depreciation of the Sanese currency against major global benchmarks, driving up the cost of imported energy and staples. This was compounded by the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of May 1–4, 2026, in which a catastrophic cascade failure in the [[Satori AI]] grid paralysed the nation for 72 hours, causing an estimated 円420 billion in economic damage and 23 indirect fatalities. The crisis led to a historic downgrade of Okaiken's sovereign credit rating from AAA to AA by the [[Adonian Credit Transparency Agency]] (ACTA)—the first such downgrade in the agency's post-war history—citing "systemic vulnerability in critical infrastructure."

==History==
{{Main|Economic history of Okaiken}}

===The merchant guilds and the Aizawa futures market===

The commercial traditions that underpin the modern Sanese economy trace to the [[Aizawa]] merchant guilds of the [[Honda Shogunate]] period (1603–1870). Despite the Shogunate's {{nihongo|"Maritime Exclusion"|海禁|Kaikin}} policy, which restricted foreign trade, the domestic economy flourished under the {{nihongo|"Great Peace"|大平|Taihei}}. The Aizawa guilds developed the world's first organized [[futures contract|futures market]]—the {{nihongo|''Aizawa Rice Exchange''|相沢米取引所|Aizawa Kome Torihikijo}}—established in 1730, nearly a century before similar institutions appeared in [[Mercia]]. The exchange standardized rice contracts, created paper substitutes for physical rice, and established a clearinghouse system that became the direct ancestor of the modern [[Okami Stock Exchange]].

===The Sanese Industrial Miracle===
[[File:Okami Factory District 1970s.png|thumb|left|250px|The Okami factory district during the height of the Sanese Industrial Miracle, c. 1970. Output doubled every five years throughout the 1960s.]]

The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1870–1871 dismantled the feudal economic order and launched a state-directed industrialization program. The government established model factories for shipbuilding, cotton spinning, and railway construction, which were subsequently sold to the emerging ''[[zaibatsu]]'' conglomerates. The nation's first railway line, connecting [[Okami]] to [[Tokuyama]] in 1876, catalyzed the development of the [[Tokuyama Industrial Belt]]—the first modern manufacturing corridor in Okaiken.

The post-[[Great Adonian War]] period produced the '''Sanese Industrial Miracle''' (1955–1975), during which real GDP growth averaged 7.5% annually. The [[Saito-era]] government directed credit through the [[Okaiken Development Bank]], prioritizing heavy industry, steel production, and shipbuilding. The [[Yonezawa Industrial Zone]], established in 1954, became the largest manufacturing complex in the capital region, while the [[Aizawa]] shipyards expanded to become the world's largest exporters of LNG carriers.

===The Restructuring Era and the digital pivot===

The 1990s {{nihongo|"Restructuring Era"|再構築時代|Saikōchiku Jidai}} saw the government aggressively intervene to pivot the economy from declining light manufacturing to high-technology and heavy industrial automation. The [[Yagikoshu]] steel and coal industries, which had employed hundreds of thousands, were systematically wound down, with the island restructured into the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]]—taking advantage of the volcanic highlands and abundant geothermal energy to host the world's largest concentration of data centers. The [[Digital Infrastructure Act]] of 2008 subsidized the creation of [[Kizan Systems]], which has since become the primary global competitor to foreign GPU manufacturers.

